# HUD overlay context menus

Source: approved context-menu discussion. Native control reference: `vsapi/Client/UI/Elements/Impl/Interactive/Controls/GuiElementListMenu.cs`.

## Goal and contract

Provide indicator settings through right-click menus while the mouse is unlocked. Retain titlebars for multi-member groups and whole-group dragging; standalone indicators have no titlebar. Reuse native list-menu rendering and interaction through a small adapter and a shared controller. Preserve existing dragging, grouping, snapping, ghost previews, cancellation, persistence, and edit-mode bounds cues.

- Indicator options: Enabled, supported Style selections, and one checkable Lock option. Checked means locked.
- Enabled and Style affect the selected indicator. Lock affects its effective container, including every member of a group.
- Disabled registered indicators remain accessible through the existing edit proxies; menus must not fabricate readings or activate unavailable integrations.
- Right-click during a drag cancels only; it must not also open a menu.
- Only multi-member groups retain titlebars for whole-group dragging. Movable corner handles, locked dashed borders, and group hover backgrounds remain.
- Reset applies only to the selected indicator; remove global reset access. Use existing layout persistence without a separate settings file. Resolve grouped-indicator reset semantics before implementation.
- Use native switch/label controls for Enabled/Lock and an embedded native Style dropdown in the same panel. Its expanded choices reuse the bounded native selection list. The native list's multi-select mode owns private switch textures without disposing them; direct native switch ownership avoids that lifetime problem without reflection or engine patches. A replacement page, Style button, mixed-action list, and cascading submenus are not required.

## Decisions to settle before removing titlebars

- [x] Retain multi-member group titlebars for whole-group movement. Ordinary member body dragging still detaches the member; no modifier gesture is introduced.
- [x] Confirm Style access: a native dropdown within the same context panel, alongside Enabled and Lock; choosing a style closes the menu.
- [x] Reset layout is a native button in the selected indicator context menu. It removes that indicator from custom membership and restores derived default group/placement and default movement permission, preserving unrelated overrides and Enabled/Style settings. Customized peers keep their layouts; untouched default peers regroup normally. No global reset is exposed. Unreadable/newer snapshots remain untouched and layout mutation actions are omitted rather than discarding unrelated data.

## Phase 1: Define shared menu capabilities and settings ownership

Outcome: menu options have explicit targets and use the same authoritative settings as existing configuration UI.

- [x] Inventory every registered indicator's enable setting, supported style enum, mutation path, persistence path, and refresh requirements.
- [x] Add optional menu capabilities to registration metadata or an adjacent registration-owned descriptor; retain compatibility with existing callers and integrations.
- [x] Expose supported actions through owning feature/configuration APIs; keep feature-specific switches out of the shared menu controller.
- [x] Distinguish configured Enabled from momentary gameplay visibility and integration availability. Omit unsupported settings rather than inventing setters.
- [x] Derive Lock from effective movement permission and translate it to the existing movement API without resetting position or changing saved layout format.
- [x] Add localized option/style labels and any group-scope explanation required to make Lock's scope clear.
- [x] Verify capabilities, enum round-trips, grouped/standalone permission scope, and persistence through focused tests.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Each supported indicator has a documented settings binding; missing capabilities remain safe and usable.
- [x] Menu changes and external configuration changes share one authoritative state.

## Phase 2: Host native popup controls independently of titlebars

Outcome: a shared popup owner can display native controls at an arbitrary pointer location.

- [x] Implement a narrow GuiElementListMenu subclass exposing protected popup measurements needed for placement, without private-field reflection or engine patches.
- [x] Implement a shared controller owning the current target, native controls/composer, focus, and disposal. Keep input policy separate from feature settings bindings.
- [x] Populate directly owned native switch/label controls from current Enabled/Lock settings; populate the separate Style selector from supported enum choices.
- [x] Account for the native list's anchor-row offset, GUI scale, scrolling, and downward expansion when calculating actual popup bounds.
- [x] Clamp the complete visible popup to every screen edge; support constrained viewports without inaccessible rows.
- [x] Close on selection as appropriate, outside click, Escape, mouse locking, HUD hiding, target removal, world/session replacement, and disposal. Keep the target stable when the pointer leaves its bounds.
- [x] Rebuild or close safely when locale, GUI scale, viewport, capabilities, or settings change. Avoid rebuilding textures every frame.
- [x] Test native-control delegation, bounds containment with synthetic InlineData geometry, toggle/selection routing, focus loss, and resource cleanup.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Popup placement and lifetime are independent of titlebar internals and transient hover geometry.
- [x] Controls use native artwork and ordinary HUD text conventions without custom mixed-menu rendering; owned switches and protected list children are disposed with the popup.

## Phase 3: Integrate pointer routing and settings changes

Outcome: indicators and edit proxies expose functional menus without breaking movement editing.

- [x] Route right-click through HudOverlayHost and controls: an active drag consumes it as cancellation before any menu-opening logic.
- [x] Hit-test visible indicator content/edit proxies deterministically, respecting foreground GUI ownership, modal input, clipping, and existing host ordering.
- [x] Capture input belonging to the open menu before drag initiation or gameplay actions; opening clicks must not also toggle a menu row.
- [x] Apply Enabled/Style using feature-owned setters and persist through existing configuration storage. Trigger the same lifecycle/refresh behavior as external configuration changes.
- [x] Keep a disabled target available as an edit proxy; handle style-dependent size changes through existing layout and attachment reflow.
- [x] Apply Lock atomically to the effective group or standalone container; refresh cues and menu state without moving the container.
- [x] Preserve keyboard navigation and dismissal without leaking Escape into unrelated cancellation or closing underlying windows.
- [x] Verify all supported styles, disabled targets, grouped scope, foreground windows, right-click cancellation, and settings changes while a menu is open.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Every advertised menu action changes the intended authoritative setting and survives its applicable restart scope.
- [x] Ordinary right-clicks outside eligible editing targets retain their existing behavior.

## Phase 4: Replace titlebars and retain movement/reset access

Outcome: standalone indicators use menus and body dragging, groups retain drag titlebars, and reset affects only the selected indicator.

- [x] Retain multi-member group titlebar dragging using existing movement transactions, snapping, source reservation, and previews; suppress singleton titlebars.
- [x] Implement approved individual Reset access, preserving unrelated indicators and persisting atomically; close stale menus after reset and remove global reset UI.
- [x] Remove standalone titlebar creation, rendering, and hit areas; remove Fixed/Movable and reset dropdown controls while retaining group captions and drag areas. Lock remains in the context menu.
- [x] Simplify the group titlebar implementation and remove unused reflection dependencies; retain position/settings persistence independently of titlebar side effects.
- [x] Preserve corner handles, locked dashed borders, hover fill, member dragging/detachment, group merges, Shift bypass, Escape/right-click cancellation, and source ghosts/reservations.
- [x] Reconcile tests against the replacement interaction contracts; remove obsolete titlebar expectations rather than keeping compatibility scaffolding.
- [x] Verify existing saved layouts load unchanged and permissions/anchors survive lock toggles, reset, and restart.

Completion criteria:

- [x] Only multi-member groups show titlebars, with no Fixed/Movable choices; standalone body dragging, member detachment, and group dragging remain accessible.
- [x] Individual reset preserves unrelated layout state and existing persistence; no global reset UI remains.

## Phase 5: Documentation, validation, and user acceptance

Outcome: implementation, current documentation, and shipped assets agree.

- [x] Update HudOverlays.md architecture/input descriptions and README user controls; document menu scope, Lock behavior, group titlebar dragging, and individual reset access. Describe current behavior only.
- [x] Audit implementation against every contract above and remove stale titlebar references, unused localization, and resources only where unreferenced.
- [x] Run focused tests and appropriate full build/test/package checks through subagents. Follow test AGENTS.md: no assertions of chosen colors, opacity, font sizes, or hardcoded visual dimensions; parameterized geometry cases supply synthetic setup values in InlineData rather than selecting them with switches inside the test body. Shared fixture geometry remains synthetic input, not an assertion of the chosen visual design.
- [x] Verify packaged localization and any required resources using canonical repository tooling.
- [x] Obtain user-run in-game acceptance: all indicators/styles and edit proxies; every screen edge and GUI scale; inventory/modal overlap; native toggles and Style selection; whole-group and individual dragging; Lock scope; reset; cancellation; restart persistence.
- [x] Record what was checked headlessly versus in game; resolve reported failures before completion.

Final acceptance:

- [x] Every approved requirement is implemented and validated, with any remaining decisions explicitly resolved.
- [x] Native popup reuse does not depend on group titlebars, duplicate settings state, or a new layout persistence implementation.
- [x] Build/test/package checks pass and user confirms the live interaction and appearance.

Verification status:

- Headless: the retained-modifier reopening regressions failed before the fix and pass afterward. Updated focused HUD tests: 630 passed; full suite: 2,733 passed. Receipts: artifacts/test-results/hud-context-menu-phase4/menu-reopen-focused.trx and menu-reopen-full.trx. Canonical Release JSON validation/build/package passed; the assembly and all 159 assets (including 28 shaders and required menu localization) match the package. Package receipts: artifacts/test-results/hud-context-menu-phase5-fix-package/.
- In game: user confirmed that reopening is fixed and all listed acceptance checks passed, including styles/edit proxies, viewport edges and GUI scales, foreground/modal behavior, settings, dragging/snapping/cancellation, individual reset, and restart persistence. The retained-key capture regression is resolved; final independent completion audit passed.
